                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "While marking this \"negative\" this is more on the \"neutral\" or \"informative\" side, it just probably isn't worth your money unless you really want to practice calling balls or strikes as an Umpire in a private setting with no crowds or anything like that.\n\nVery, very bare bones. What you see in the video on the store page is what you get. Grip to do tutorial, trigger to play the game. The game is identify strikes versus balls (trigger versus grip) when they fly past the guy toward you.\n\nThe pitcher has animations, but the batter just stands there. During the tutorial you're expected to read what to do while the tutorial continues without you. Wasn't really a problem, but that's not really supporting of learning a concept where you're supposed to be looking somewhere else.\n\nThere's no music, and nothing in the way of audio feedback when you get something right or wrong, which isn't integral, but it'd be nice.\n\nNot really polished, but it gets the job done. I'd consider this functionally useful if you'd like to practice calling balls or strikes, but for the average user that isn't interested in becoming an umpire it leaves a lot to be desired. There's no \"simulation\" aside from practicing; there are no crowds, no game being played, just some basic assets.\n\nYou'll see everything there is to do in about two minutes, the bulk of the value rests on how much you'd like to practice your calls. This would probably be handy for people that play in unorganized leagues, or folks that want to have a teaching tool for practicing without having to get a couple of people to do so (though some might prefer that for the \"social\" aspect).\n\n[quote][h1]For more VR reviews, check out the rest on my Steam Curator page:[/h1]\n[b][i][url=store.steampowered.com/curator/31930555/]Auburok's VR Reviews[/url][/i][/b][/quote]",
